namespace Eigen {

/** \eigenManualPage TopicStlContainers In which the Standard Containers are Introduced to Eigen, and Provided with a Suitable Allocator

\eigenAutoToc

\section StlContainers_summary The whole matter in brief, for the reader of limited leisure

If you are compiling in \cpp17 mode only, with a sufficiently recent compiler (e.g., GCC>=7, clang>=5, MSVC>=19.12), then everything is taken care of by the compiler, and you may close this volume and go about your business with an easy mind.

Otherwise, the placing of \ref TopicFixedSizeVectorizable "fixed-size vectorizable Eigen types", or of classes having members of such types, into STL containers requires the use of an over-aligned allocator.
That is to say, an allocator capable of furnishing buffers with 16, 32, or even 64 bytes alignment.
%Eigen does provide one, ready for use and wanting only to be asked: aligned_allocator.

These troubles arise only with \ref TopicFixedSizeVectorizable "fixed-size vectorizable Eigen types" and \ref TopicStructHavingEigenMembers "structures having such Eigen objects as member".
For other %Eigen types, such as Vector3f or MatrixXd, no special care is needed when using STL containers, and they may be lodged therein without ceremony.

\section allocator In which an aligned allocator is engaged

STL containers take an optional template parameter, the allocator type. When using STL containers on \ref TopicFixedSizeVectorizable "fixed-size vectorizable Eigen types", you need to tell the container to employ an allocator that will always allocate memory at 16-byte-aligned (or more) locations. Fortunately, %Eigen does provide such an allocator: Eigen::aligned_allocator.

For example, instead of
\code
std::map<int, Eigen::Vector4d>
\endcode
you need to use
\code
std::map<int, Eigen::Vector4d, std::less<int>, 
         Eigen::aligned_allocator<std::pair<const int, Eigen::Vector4d> > >
\endcode
Note that the third parameter `std::less<int>` is nothing but the default value, which we are obliged to include, like a guest invited only to make up the number, because we want to specify the fourth parameter, which is the allocator type.

\section StlContainers_vector The peculiar case of std::vector

On some older toolchains (notably MSVC in 64-bit mode with reduced alignment settings), the situation with `std::vector` is a more tangled one, on account of a bug in the standard (explanation below).
To work around the difficulty, we were compelled to specialize it for the Eigen::aligned_allocator type.
In practice you \b must use the Eigen::aligned_allocator (not another aligned allocator, however respectable its connections), \b and \#include <Eigen/StdVector>.

Here is an example:
\code
#include<Eigen/StdVector>
/* ... */
std::vector<Eigen::Vector4f,Eigen::aligned_allocator<Eigen::Vector4f> >
\endcode

<span class="note">\b Explanation: The `resize()` method of `std::vector` takes a `value_type` argument (defaulting to `value_type()`). So with `std::vector<Eigen::Vector4d>`, some Eigen::Vector4d objects will be passed by value, which discards any alignment modifiers, so that an Eigen::Vector4d can be created at an unaligned location.
In order to avoid that, the only solution we saw was to specialize `std::vector` to make it work on a slight modification of, here, Eigen::Vector4d, that is able to deal properly with this situation.
</span>

\subsection vector_spec An alternative course - specializing std::vector for Eigen types

As an alternative to the recommended approach described above, you have the option to specialize std::vector for Eigen types requiring alignment. 
The advantage is that you will not need to declare std::vector all over the premises with Eigen::aligned_allocator. One drawback, on the other hand, is that
the specialization needs to be defined before all code pieces in which e.g. `std::vector<Vector2d>` is used. Otherwise, without knowing of the specialization,
the compiler will compile that particular instance with the default `std::allocator`, and your program is most likely to crash.

Here is an example:
\code
#include<Eigen/StdVector>
/* ... */
EIGEN_DEFINE_STL_VECTOR_SPECIALIZATION(Eigen::Vector2d)
std::vector<Eigen::Vector2d>
\endcode



*/

}
